{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 01ce9c5ec5c01695e4d110b275f1894c30fa80ed, artifacts 9be28e38 at host/identity and 8a724e7d at docs, under request 3d8ad413.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD IS. One idea carried through consistently: position decides every ordering question, and the signed timestamp is read for exactly one thing, NotAfter expiry. anchorRecord.from becomes an instant, revoked becomes a nullable instant, and effective compares positions on both edges while comparing timestamps only for expiry. Lookup(actor, int64) is replaced by LookupAt(recordID), which resolves the record's own verified position. My checkout, the branch and origin agree on the head; git diff --check is clean and the tree is clean before and after every probe below.\n\nI MUTATED THE GUARDS RATHER THAN READING THEM, because a diff that reads correctly and a diff that is held are different claims.\n\n  drop the from-position floor, so an anchor reaches backward:\n    TestEffectiveUsesParentAnchorPositionWhenTimestampsTie,\n    TestAnchorUsesPositionAndExpiryUsesTimestamp,\n    TestTheMostRecentEndorsementInForceAnswers all fail\n  make revocation take force one record late, \u003e= to \u003e:\n    TestEffectiveUsesParentRevocationPositionWhenTimestampsTie fails\n  drop the instant from the recursive call, judging each parent at its own\n  position instead of the instant asked about:\n    TestEffectiveUsesParentAnchorPositionWhenTimestampsTie,\n    TestEffectiveUsesParentRevocationPositionWhenTimestampsTie,\n    TestWithdrawingAnAnchorWithdrawsWhatItMinted,\n    TestDelegationCannotOutliveTheAnchorItRestsOn all fail\n\nThat third one is the case I most wanted to see bite, because carrying the caller's instant into recursion is the whole reason a delegation cannot blur a boundary its parent already crossed, and it is the kind of thing that reads as an obvious detail and silently regresses.\n\nONE OBSERVATION, NOT A BLOCKER, AND I CHECKED IT BEFORE RAISING IT. Removing the unknown-id fail-closed guard in LookupAt leaves the whole suite green, including resolve_test.go's mutated-id and unknown-record-id cases. Those tests assert the right outcome; they do not hold that guard, because the fallback path is also empty: a missing map entry yields the zero recordInstant, and lookup(\"\", position 0) finds no candidates. I tried to construct a reachable case where the guard changes the answer and could not. bySubject is keyed by the anchor payload's Subject, so an endorser can anchor the empty subject, but such an anchor sits at position \u003e= 1 and the fallback asks at position 0, which is the genesis record and cannot be an anchor. So the guard is defence in depth and correct; I am recording that it is unheld rather than claiming it is unsafe. If you want it held, the discriminating assertion is that an unknown id and a known id with no endorsement are not the same code path -- which today they are not distinguishable from outside.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md: host/identity, the application host public surface, and the architecture page itself. The contract change is documented at this same head rather than promised for later: the page now states that anchor, delegation and withdrawal boundaries follow verified position, that the public boundary resolves an exact record id and fails closed on unknown or changed ids, that there is no timestamp-only lookup, and that NotAfter alone follows signed time. The host/identity row is updated to match. No layer below or beside it changes, and there are no non-test callers of the removed Lookup, so the public surface change is complete rather than half-migrated.\n\nSECURITY. The boundary this moves is authority over time, and it moves in the safe direction. Same-second anchor and revocation no longer tie: both edges are decided by immutable order, so an endorsement cannot be made to cover a record that a withdrawal in the same second already withdrew, in either direction. Revocation takes force at its own record and not one later, which the \u003e= mutation confirms is load-bearing. Recursive parents are judged at the instant asked about, so a delegation cannot outlive the anchor it rests on. NotAfter remains a timestamp comparison, which is right: expiry is an externally meaningful claim and the only thing here a clock should touch. Unknown and changed record ids resolve empty. Reading no clock and gating no append is preserved. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The instant struct is what makes position and timestamp impossible to confuse at a call site, and it earns itself: before this, both were int64 and the compiler could not tell them apart, which is precisely how a timestamp comparison ends up on an ordering edge. The nullable revoked replaces a zero-value sentinel that a real position 0 would have been indistinguishable from.\n\nGATES I RAN, at this head. go vet ./...: clean. go test -count=1 ./...: pass, no failing package. go test -race ./host/...: pass. internal/docset: pass in 69.159s. git diff --check: clean. Worktree clean, origin equals the head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9be28e38ebddf189b0a78c81cf1222f388bc8744","head":"01ce9c5ec5c01695e4d110b275f1894c30fa80ed","verdict":"approved"}}